<Section title="Editorial standards" description="How Mind Assembly prepares, reviews and corrects psychology writing.">
  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>We welcome psychology explainers, research commentary and student perspectives. Authors should distinguish established findings, emerging evidence, theory and personal interpretation. Our readings are for education and discussion. Mind Assembly does not provide diagnosis, treatment or clinical services.</p>
  <h2>Editorial review</h2>
  <p>Before assigning a reviewed status, an editor should check the sources, the accuracy of the interpretation, the stated limitations and any permissions or conflicts of interest. Credit authors and reviewers by name with their consent. Editorial review describes an internal editorial check.</p>
  <h2>Contributor guidelines</h2>
  <p>Send an outline with the question you want to answer, your intended audience and a starting source. Cite original studies where possible, give enough bibliographic information to locate each source, and link to its DOI or publisher. Use APA 7 for formal reference lists. Obtain permission for material you reproduce.</p>
  <p><a href="/join.html">Pitch a contribution</a></p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:mindassembly2026@gmail.com?subject=Editorial%20correction">Mind Assembly</a> with the reading URL, the issue and any supporting source. When a substantive correction is made, record what changed and the date on the reading.</p>
  <h2>Research ethics</h2>
  <p>Projects involving participants should identify the responsible research team, seek applicable review before recruitment and explain consent, confidentiality and data handling. Project entries should state their status and identify any verified approvals or affiliations.</p>
</Section>
